 "title": "Transfer curve of a Mach–Zehnder modulator",
 "caption": "The voltage shifts the phase in one arm, so the two arms interfere: T = cos²(πV/2V_π). With V_π = 3 V, the output is fully on at 0 V, half at 1.5 V (the quadrature point, where modulation is most linear) and off at 3 V.",
 "alt": "Transmitted fraction against voltage, a cosine-squared curve from 1 at 0 volts through 0.5 at 1.5 volts to 0 at 3 volts and back up.",
 "description": [
  "T = cos²(πV / (2 × 3 V)).",
  "V = 0: T = 1. V = 1.5 V: cos²(π/4) = 0.5. V = 3 V: T = 0.",
  "Around 1.5 V the curve is steepest and nearly straight, the usual bias point."
